# Nanobrowser Project - Windsurf Guidelines

## Package Manager & Commands
- Always use `pnpm` for package management (required)
- Use workspace-scoped commands: `pnpm -F <workspace> <command>`
- Prefer targeted builds: `pnpm -F chrome-extension build` over root builds

## Development Workflow
- Run `pnpm type-check` before committing
- Use `pnpm lint` for code quality checks
- Test with `pnpm -F chrome-extension test` for unit tests
- Build extension with `pnpm build` before testing

## Code Style Requirements
- Use 2 spaces for indentation (Prettier config)
- Follow ESLint rules (React, TypeScript, Import, A11y)
- Components: PascalCase, variables/functions: camelCase
- Use `import type` for type-only imports

## Architecture Constraints
- Monorepo with Turbo - respect workspace boundaries
- Chrome Extension Manifest V3 - no Manifest V2 code
- Background scripts run as service workers
- Use Chrome APIs for browser automation

## Multi-Agent System
- Three agents: Navigator, Planner, Validator
- Agent logic in `chrome-extension/src/background/agent/`
- Use Chrome messaging APIs for coordination

## Security Requirements
- Never hardcode API keys or credentials
- Use VITE_ prefixed environment variables
- Validate all user inputs
- Follow Content Security Policy

## Testing Guidelines
- Write unit tests for business logic
- Use Vitest framework
- Tests in `__tests__` directories with `*.test.ts`
- Mock network/browser APIs

## Internationalization
- Use structured naming: `component_category_specificAction_state`
- Prefixes: `bg_` (background), `exec_` (executor), `act_` (actions)
- Placeholders with Chrome i18n format
- Edit source JSON in `packages/i18n/locales/`

## Build & Distribution
- Extension builds to `dist/` directory
- Distribution zips in `dist-zip/`
- Do not edit generated files
- Use `__DEV__` flag for development builds

## Performance Guidelines
- Use array methods (map, filter, reduce)
- Implement memoization for expensive calls
- Minimize DOM manipulations
- Use React optimization (useMemo, useCallback)

## File Organization
- Extract reusable logic into utilities
- Keep functions small and focused
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Use dependency injection for testability

## Windsurf-Specific Best Practices

### Context Management
- Use `@filename` or `@symbol` to pin specific files instead of scanning entire project
- Start fresh sessions for new tasks to maintain context accuracy
- Clear chat history when switching contexts (e.g., React to Node.js)
- Monitor context window indicator - start new session when red
- Close unnecessary tabs (Fast Context engine prioritizes open files)

### Planning & Execution
- Use `/plan` command for architectural decisions and complex tasks
- Follow plan-review-execute loop to save tokens
- Break down large tasks: "Build dashboard" → "/plan Create dashboard layout"
- Use `/execute` for implementation phases
- Use `megaplan` for complex architectural changes
- Set reasoning effort: Low/Medium for routine, High for complex logic
- Use Arena Mode to compare models side-by-side

### MCP Server Integration
- Available servers: `@sqlite`, `@github`, `@playwright`, `@web`
- Use `@web Search` for latest API documentation
- Use `@github Create PR` for repository management
- Use `@sqlite Query` for local data operations
- MCP configurations in `.windsurf/mcp-examples/`

### Terminal Execution
- Use Cascade Dedicated Terminal for reliable command execution
- Commands can handle interactive prompts
- Prefer workspace-scoped commands with `pnpm -F <workspace>`
- Use batched commands for efficiency

### Memory Management
- Review and delete outdated AI memories regularly
- Archive completed project plans separately
- Keep active memories limited to current sprint
- Categories: Active, Archive, Reference, Temporary

### Team Collaboration
- Windsurf handles architect role (planning, structure)
- VS Code/Copilot handles developer role (implementation)
- Shared context files: `CLAUDE.md`, `.windsurfrules`, `TODO.md`
- Use structured PR comments with @mentions for specific files
- Clear role distribution for complex features

### Token Optimization
- Batch similar requests (@src/agent/ Review all agent files)
- Use structured prompts (/plan, /execute, /test)
- Avoid long conversations and full project scans
- Keep .windsurfrules under 6,000 tokens (~4,500 words)
